Thanks all
I’ve accepted in most of those amends, and made a few more (though largely cosmetic). The reference to the section in the CMH1 judgment about the aggregate total of Ds’ costs was deliberate, just
 because I think it is important to remind the wider cohort of firms why the totals look so different between Ds and Cs.
&nbsp;
A few responses to some of the comments below in case helpful:
Sophia:

In terms of the SFC directions, if you think the sealed version will be out on Monday, fine to wait rather than circulating the draft. Otherwise, I think
 it makes sense to circulate the final draft just to make extra sure everyone has a copy (given the deadline next Friday).On Pan-NOx recoveries, I don’t think we can safely go further than [21] at this stage (which is essentially in the same terms as last year’s note). It is
 necessarily uncertain, as is going to have to be approach commercially/on a case-by-case basis in the event of settlements. It seems to be that we can’t say more about allocation at this stage because the court is using it as a category precisely because it
 wants to avoid any conclusions about allocation at this stage.I’m minded not to raise the ‘Olie Lawson’ point because it is really a point which only applies to Milberg, and I am sure they will have read the judgment
 and taken note of that point. I think adding it in to a Note for everyone is just going to look like singling them out.
Sarah:

I’ve just deleted that [28]-[30] section you were worried about. It was an important point to make last year but, on reflection, I expect it has been overtaken
 somewhat by the conversations which have been had since then and the further arrangements put in place for ‘sharing’ the pot (or not, as the case may be), so better just to leave it out now.Re your comment on the way to deal with Pan-NOx divisions etc, I agree those are probably the main options, but I think the problem with it is that the examples
 work on the assumption of all Cs being entitled to recover their costs. In a scenario where some Cs are entitled to recover their costs but others are not, the ‘access’ to the budgeted sums would necessarily shift (at least formally, for the purposes of which
 Ds which costs were sought from). It might be that the same principles could be applied, even if to a different set of claimant groups, or that sums are shared internally in a way which is different to the way that they are strictly recovered from the other
 side. In any event, I certainly agree with you that it’s not something we are really able to get into in this Note – it is a much more difficult question for another day.On Non-Lead time, I have added a line in [33] to highlight the fact that the court refused to countenance any more than “extremely limited” costs from Non-Leads
 in these budgets, and that this echoed the approach exhibited at CMH1 (even if not necessarily apparent in the CMH1 judgment). I’m not sure I can go further than that as clearly they have accounted for some Non-Lead costs in there, just extremely limited sums
 (though, again, I have a degree of sympathy with the NLs there because I still don’t think the court has fully appreciated the full extent of what counts as ‘common costs’, and wrongly assumes that ‘individual costs’ are the solution).On SteerCo time, the position is trickier because in principle there is nothing to stop a lot of work being delegated to SteerCo firms and it being recovered
