Hi Jemma,&nbsp;Please see the correspondence attached with an imminent leaver, Matthew Hunt. &nbsp;I have also attached similar correspondence with another leaver, Michael Burke, to give you further context regarding the sickness policy piece/queries we are getting from leavers who are going off sick.&nbsp;Additonally, I have attached a copy of Matt&#8217;s employment contract and the Sickness Absence Policy.&nbsp;The part I would like you advice on please is the extract below:&nbsp;&#8220;Eligibility for OSP&nbsp;I see from HiBob that Natasha uploaded a sickness policy on 17 November 2025 at 09:59:39.&nbsp; The term that you rely on is the fifth OSP condition under Section 7 (Occupational Sick Pay), so I will refer to it as OSP condition 7(5).&nbsp; There was no message to staff that the policy was updated/ launched.&nbsp;- Is the firm's position that my employment contract automatically incorporates all terms of all firm policies, even if they are not drawn to my attention, and whatever the term?- Is the firm's position that my employment contract incorporates OSP condition 7(5) even though it was not drawn to my attention, and despite me repeatedly drawing to the firm's attention my health (see, for example, my resignation letter and formal grievance)?- Could you please send me a copy of the previous policy, if there was one?&nbsp; If there is a previous policy but you decline to send it to me, please confirm whether OSP condition 7(5) was in the previous policy.- If OSP condition 7(5) was not in the previous policy, or there was no previous policy, please explain the reason for the policy update/ launch.- You say that &quot;employees in comparable circumstances, i.e., those where notices has been given, receive SSP rather than OSP.&quot;&nbsp; Are you positively stating that there are no&nbsp;PG staff who have resigned and are currently working their notice period, who throughout that notice period have received OSP, including before 17 November 2025?&#8221;&nbsp;I have drafted the following, but I wanted to check this with you, please. Is there anything you would advise I amend to make this response as robust as possible?&nbsp;&#8220;2. Occupational Sick Pay (OSP)The firm&#8217;s employment contracts incorporate terms as outlined in company policies. Employees are expected to review updated policies, which are published on HiBob.The sickness policy uploaded on 17 November 2025 at 09:59:39 was the first iteration of a formal policy. There was no previous sickness policy in place.OSP condition 7(5) was included in this first iteration. The policy was published to clarify eligibility for occupational sick pay going forward.The firm confirms that employees serving notice periods, including those who have resigned, receive SSP rather than OSP, consistent with anyone else who takes a period of sick leave after this policy was implemented.&#8221;&nbsp;Feel free to give me a call tomorrow, once you have had the opportunity to review the above and attachments.&nbsp;Thank you,Tash "}

