&nbsp;

There are a number of foreign lawyers who are said to be Grade A/B fee earners and as such have claimed rates in line with those grades.&nbsp; However,
 the guidelines are clear in specifying that those who can claim a Grade A and B rates are
“Solicitors and legal executives with over 8(or 4 for Grade B) years’ experience”.&nbsp; Therefore, in these circumstances we usually state that the fee earners are a Grade C but claim the Grade A rate.&nbsp; However, in this case, we do not believe that this
 is possible as assume the retainer would specify the Grade and the rate to be charged rather than specific fee earners.&nbsp; As a result, if we are to keep them claimed at a Grade A rate, it is likely that this will be criticised by the other side.&nbsp; However, if
 we claim them at the Grade C rate, this will reduce the costs claim.&nbsp; Please can you advise on how you wish to proceed.
[I am bit puzzled by this comment, because it implies that we may be claiming a hypothetical rather than an actual rate in respect of foreign lawyers. Should we not just be claiming the actual rates charged by the foreign lawyers? The
 question whether that was a reasonable rate and in line with guideline rates is a matter for assessment, but I don’t think that we need to second guess this at this stage. Likewise, I assume that for English solicitors, we are claiming by reference to the
